WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.266
- Hello, Believe Nation,
I'm Evan Charmichael.

00:00:05.266 --> 00:00:06.511
My one word is believe.

00:00:06.511 --> 00:00:09.007
And I believe that
entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:09.007 --> 00:00:11.259
all of the world's major problems.

00:00:11.259 --> 00:00:13.574
I started the Mentor Me Series with a goal

00:00:13.574 --> 00:00:16.138
to try to learn from people
who've done a lot more than us,

00:00:16.138 --> 00:00:17.946
people who have done amazing things,

00:00:17.946 --> 00:00:20.188
that by spending a little
bit more time with them,

00:00:20.188 --> 00:00:22.629
hopefully, some of their
values, their belief systems,

00:00:22.629 --> 00:00:24.742
their way of thinking, seeps into us

00:00:24.742 --> 00:00:27.547
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:27.547 --> 00:00:29.326
So to help you on your journey today,

00:00:29.326 --> 00:00:30.858
we're going to learn from Robin Sharma

00:00:30.858 --> 00:00:33.334
and some of his best
leadership motivation.

00:00:33.334 --> 00:00:34.341
Mentor me, Robin.

00:00:34.341 --> 00:00:35.930
And, as always, guys, if you're watching

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:37.578
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:37.578 --> 00:00:39.754
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:39.754 --> 00:00:41.571
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:41.571 --> 00:00:42.836
can be inspired as well.

00:00:42.836 --> 00:00:45.433
And when you write it down,
it's much more likely to stick

00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:47.364
with yourself, too.

00:00:47.364 --> 00:00:48.502
Enjoy.

00:00:48.502 --> 00:00:51.335
(uplifting music)

00:00:59.996 --> 00:01:03.853
- I've spent so much of the
past 20 years of my life

00:01:03.853 --> 00:01:08.439
evangelizing a message that
leadership is not about a title,

00:01:08.439 --> 00:01:11.386
leadership is not about
the size of your office.

00:01:11.386 --> 00:01:13.996
Leadership is not about
having formal authority.

00:01:13.996 --> 00:01:15.448
Leadership is a mindset.

00:01:15.448 --> 00:01:18.551
Leadership is a way of
operating through your days.

00:01:18.551 --> 00:01:20.200
Leadership is a heartset.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.371
Leadership is a way of being.

00:01:22.371 --> 00:01:25.157
And I think we're now in Leadership 2.0.

00:01:25.157 --> 00:01:27.209
The old model of leadership said

00:01:27.209 --> 00:01:28.791
you need to have a lot of money,

00:01:28.791 --> 00:01:31.097
or you need to be a prime
minister or a president.

00:01:31.097 --> 00:01:33.702
You need to be a CEO
or a managing director

00:01:33.702 --> 00:01:35.734
in order to be a dealer.

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:39.112
Leadership 2.0 is fundamentally different.

00:01:39.112 --> 00:01:41.197
Leadership 2.0 simply says

00:01:41.197 --> 00:01:44.649
if you can breathe, if you are alive,

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:46.888
you not only have the opportunity,

00:01:46.888 --> 00:01:49.547
you have the responsibility
to show leadership,

00:01:49.547 --> 00:01:52.173
not only in your work, not
only in your creativity,

00:01:52.173 --> 00:01:54.949
not only in your impact,
not only in your influence,

00:01:54.949 --> 00:01:56.861
but even in your personal life.

00:01:56.861 --> 00:01:58.513
So let's get right to the first tactic

00:01:58.513 --> 00:02:00.082
for you to be an even better leader.

00:02:00.082 --> 00:02:03.998
Install the lead with a title mindset.

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:05.821
And, again, that's just
a away of thinking.

00:02:05.821 --> 00:02:07.133
It's an approach.

00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:10.577
So what I'm really suggesting
is no matter what you do,

00:02:10.577 --> 00:02:12.798
no matter where you station in life is,

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:15.689
start thinking like a leader,

00:02:15.689 --> 00:02:17.270
because you really have a choice

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.601
when you go out in the world every day.

00:02:18.601 --> 00:02:20.358
You can be a victim or
you can be a leader,

00:02:20.358 --> 00:02:21.981
but you can't do both.

00:02:21.981 --> 00:02:25.351
And so the more you
practice showing leadership

00:02:25.351 --> 00:02:27.591
in your work, showing
leadership with your family,

00:02:27.591 --> 00:02:29.386
showing leadership in your community,

00:02:29.386 --> 00:02:31.608
showing leadership in your private self.

00:02:31.608 --> 00:02:34.151
Practice drives performance.

00:02:34.151 --> 00:02:35.401
And the more you practice it,

00:02:35.401 --> 00:02:38.180
the more you start to
step in to the mindset

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.654
of a leader without a title.

00:02:39.654 --> 00:02:42.250
Our wold says that if you are a leader,

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.762
in many ways, it's about what you get.

00:02:44.762 --> 00:02:48.089
What I'm going to suggest
to you, with deep respect,

00:02:48.089 --> 00:02:50.149
being a leader is about what you give.

00:02:50.149 --> 00:02:54.280
And it's a paradox,
because as you give more,

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.152
you actually receive more.

00:02:56.152 --> 00:03:00.094
But, again, society as
sold us this methodology

00:03:00.094 --> 00:03:04.635
and this philosophy that
if we get from the world,

00:03:04.635 --> 00:03:07.897
if we get more income, if we
get more from our clients,

00:03:07.897 --> 00:03:09.977
if we get more from our employees,

00:03:09.977 --> 00:03:12.520
if get more from our sectors,

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:13.946
if we get more from society,

00:03:13.946 --> 00:03:17.707
we will feel happy, we will be successful,

00:03:17.707 --> 00:03:20.045
we will be at the top of the mountain,

00:03:20.045 --> 00:03:23.675
we will live these epic,
awesome and fulfilling lives.

00:03:23.675 --> 00:03:25.548
And I don't believe that's the case.

00:03:25.548 --> 00:03:27.081
And I haven't experienced
that to be the case at all.

00:03:27.081 --> 00:03:31.755
To me, if you give, and
you are a humble instrument

00:03:31.755 --> 00:03:34.380
of service, everything
just falls into place.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.680
And so what I wanted to do is really

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.445
start off by talking
about the new economy.

00:03:39.445 --> 00:03:41.997
The old economy was an
economy of scarcity.

00:03:41.997 --> 00:03:44.380
It was like there's
not enough in the world

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:48.717
so how can I get as much as
possible before it's all gone.

00:03:48.717 --> 00:03:52.411
The new economy that we do business in

00:03:52.411 --> 00:03:54.494
and that we live life in,

00:03:55.479 --> 00:03:57.174
is one not of scarcity
but it's about generosity.

00:03:57.174 --> 00:04:00.107
And I think the person
who gives the most wins.

00:04:00.107 --> 00:04:02.395
And that's just the world
we live in right now,

00:04:02.395 --> 00:04:04.111
whether you believe this or not.

00:04:04.111 --> 00:04:06.102
So what I'm going to
challenge you to do is simply,

00:04:06.102 --> 00:04:08.075
I'm going to walk you through
some insights right now.

00:04:08.075 --> 00:04:10.369
But start thinking about this.

00:04:10.369 --> 00:04:12.925
What if Robin is right?

00:04:12.925 --> 00:04:15.866
The strangest paradox of leadership

00:04:15.866 --> 00:04:17.930
is not about what I give.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:19.995
The strangest paradox of leadership

00:04:19.995 --> 00:04:23.018
is as I serve more and help more people,

00:04:23.018 --> 00:04:25.931
I not only become more,
I actually receive more.

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:29.213
The first insight I want to offer to you

00:04:29.213 --> 00:04:31.005
and I call it the 10X Value Obsession.

00:04:31.005 --> 00:04:35.172
Just make a brain tattoo,
sort of a psychological wiring

00:04:36.334 --> 00:04:38.923
that you practice until the point

00:04:38.923 --> 00:04:41.025
it's unconscious and automatic,

00:04:41.025 --> 00:04:42.058
which is this.

00:04:42.058 --> 00:04:45.657
How can I give my customers
the 10 times the value

00:04:45.657 --> 00:04:48.091
that they have any right to expect?

00:04:48.091 --> 00:04:51.180
So whatever it is, whatever
product you pour into the world,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.282
how can that product give
them 10 times the value

00:04:54.282 --> 00:04:55.915
that they're expecting.

00:04:55.915 --> 00:04:59.248
That dominant mindset and way of being

00:04:59.248 --> 00:05:01.877
has colossal consequences.

00:05:01.877 --> 00:05:03.734
You see, most people can talk the talk,

00:05:03.734 --> 00:05:05.254
they don't walk it.

00:05:05.254 --> 00:05:07.078
They talk the talk, "Yeah, I want to give.

00:05:07.078 --> 00:05:08.425
"I want to wow my customers.

00:05:08.425 --> 00:05:09.923
"I want to give great products.

00:05:09.923 --> 00:05:11.303
"I want to give a great user experience,

00:05:11.303 --> 00:05:12.790
"great customer service."

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:14.369
Maybe even to their friends.

00:05:14.369 --> 00:05:18.038
They talk a good game,
but there's a misalignment

00:05:18.038 --> 00:05:21.927
between their vocabulary that
they're expressing publicly

00:05:21.927 --> 00:05:25.047
and their actual internal intent.

00:05:25.047 --> 00:05:27.927
And when there is a
misalignment with what you say

00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:29.495
and what you truly feel,

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:31.703
even if what you truly
feel is subconscious,

00:05:31.703 --> 00:05:33.802
you don't even know it, people feel it.

00:05:33.802 --> 00:05:36.902
So the first idea on
this 10X Value Obsession,

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:40.935
you really want to come from a
place where you actually feel,

00:05:40.935 --> 00:05:43.237
"I need to be an instrument of service.

00:05:43.237 --> 00:05:47.031
"Robin is right, I want to
be a giver versus a taker.

00:05:47.031 --> 00:05:50.743
"I want to give these people
who put food on my table,

00:05:50.743 --> 00:05:54.092
"that I call my customers,
10 times the value

00:05:54.092 --> 00:05:55.798
"they have any right to expect."

00:05:55.798 --> 00:05:59.002
And once that becomes your emotional

00:05:59.002 --> 00:06:01.706
and your dominant way of
being, people feel it.

00:06:01.706 --> 00:06:03.345
And it changes your behavior.

00:06:03.345 --> 00:06:04.785
And it changes your performance.

00:06:04.785 --> 00:06:06.515
You actually go the extra mile.

00:06:06.515 --> 00:06:07.660
You actually start to wow.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:09.486
Your products are not mediocre,

00:06:09.486 --> 00:06:11.596
your products are masterful.

00:06:11.596 --> 00:06:14.444
Your team is not average,
your team becomes iconic.

00:06:14.444 --> 00:06:15.442
Why?

00:06:15.442 --> 00:06:17.274
Because you're coming
from that deep emotional,

00:06:17.274 --> 00:06:20.444
passionate place that is far more powerful

00:06:20.444 --> 00:06:22.327
than the cold hard logic of,

00:06:22.327 --> 00:06:24.204
"Let us make our numbers

00:06:24.204 --> 00:06:27.371
"so that we satisfy our shareholders."

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:33.275
Number three, I call it
the three I practice,

00:06:33.275 --> 00:06:35.306
the three I practice.

00:06:35.306 --> 00:06:38.198
If you want to be a
better leader, understand,

00:06:38.198 --> 00:06:40.346
and then practice these three Is.

00:06:40.346 --> 00:06:42.139
Number one, inspiration.

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:46.097
The job of a leader really
is to inspire other people.

00:06:46.097 --> 00:06:48.653
If you look at Steve Jobs,
you look at Elon Musk,

00:06:48.653 --> 00:06:50.517
you look at Nelson Mandela,

00:06:50.517 --> 00:06:52.925
you look at Sergey Brin of Google,

00:06:52.925 --> 00:06:56.406
you look at a Rembrandt or a Picasso,

00:06:56.406 --> 00:07:00.342
these people were incredibly inspired.

00:07:00.342 --> 00:07:01.956
They not only were incredibly inspired,

00:07:01.956 --> 00:07:04.661
they were inspirational to other people.

00:07:04.661 --> 00:07:06.855
I mean, there's that
incredible story of Steve Jobs.

00:07:06.855 --> 00:07:10.441
And a lot of people say Steve
Jobs wasn't a nice person.

00:07:10.441 --> 00:07:14.862
And, yet, if you were to poll
the former employees of Apple,

00:07:14.862 --> 00:07:17.385
they would say, "My years at
Apple, working with Steve Jobs,

00:07:17.385 --> 00:07:20.636
"were the single best
years of my entire career."

00:07:20.636 --> 00:07:21.635
And you say, "Why?"

00:07:21.635 --> 00:07:24.474
And they say, "Because
he was inspirational."

00:07:24.474 --> 00:07:28.310
I mean, when Steve Jobs was
a young man, or a young kid,

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:30.361
his father said, "Go out
and paint the fence."

00:07:30.361 --> 00:07:31.851
outside of their house.

00:07:31.851 --> 00:07:33.860
And Steve Jobs said, "No problem."

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:35.098
He went out and he started painting

00:07:35.098 --> 00:07:36.693
the outside of the fence.

00:07:36.693 --> 00:07:38.538
A few hours later, his father comes out

00:07:38.538 --> 00:07:40.268
and says, "Steve, did
you paint the fence?"

00:07:40.268 --> 00:07:42.379
Steve says, "I did, dad."

00:07:42.379 --> 00:07:44.808
Steve Jobs' father inspects the fence.

00:07:44.808 --> 00:07:47.464
And he says, "Steve, you
did a great job painting

00:07:47.464 --> 00:07:49.201
"the outside of the fence,
but you didn't paint

00:07:49.201 --> 00:07:50.595
"the inside of the fence."

00:07:50.595 --> 00:07:52.545
And Steve Jobs looks up
at his father and says,

00:07:52.545 --> 00:07:57.073
"But, dad, no one's going to
see the inside of the fence."

00:07:57.073 --> 00:08:01.240
And his father looks at him
and says, "But, son, we will."

00:08:02.332 --> 00:08:05.744
Now it's many decades later
and Steve Jobs is running Apple

00:08:05.744 --> 00:08:07.287
and they're ready to launch,

00:08:07.287 --> 00:08:10.385
or ready to develop, rather,
the first Apple Macintosh.

00:08:10.385 --> 00:08:12.589
And he looks at his
design team and he said,

00:08:12.589 --> 00:08:14.072
"Here's your deliverable.

00:08:14.072 --> 00:08:18.297
"I want the outside of the
computer to look so simple

00:08:18.297 --> 00:08:20.390
"and so elegant like a piece of art.

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:23.560
"But here's the real mission.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:25.977
"Make the inside so beautiful

00:08:27.097 --> 00:08:29.945
"that it'll bring tears to our eyes."

00:08:29.945 --> 00:08:31.670
And his design team
looked at him and said,

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:33.247
"But, Steve, no one's
going to see the inside

00:08:33.247 --> 00:08:35.695
"of the Apple Macintosh."

00:08:35.695 --> 00:08:40.108
And Steve Jobs looked at his
team and said, "But we will."

00:08:40.108 --> 00:08:41.535
That's inspiration.

00:08:41.535 --> 00:08:44.320
I think the only title
that really matters is CIO,

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:46.653
Chief Inspirational Officer.

00:08:46.653 --> 00:08:48.511
So no matter what you do,
whether you're a teacher,

00:08:48.511 --> 00:08:51.161
or a firefighter, an
astronaut, or an entrepreneur.

00:08:51.161 --> 00:08:52.863
Maybe you're a managing director,

00:08:52.863 --> 00:08:54.639
maybe you're a mother or father.

00:08:54.639 --> 00:08:58.336
Be the CIO, the Chief
Inspirational Officer,

00:08:58.336 --> 00:08:59.679
of all you do.

00:08:59.679 --> 00:09:01.633
The second I of the three I practice

00:09:01.633 --> 00:09:05.800
to make you an amazing leader
is influence, influence.

00:09:07.357 --> 00:09:10.335
You want to be so good at what you do

00:09:10.335 --> 00:09:12.159
that you influence other people.

00:09:12.159 --> 00:09:16.030
You want to use the words and
the language of leadership

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:18.384
versus victim speak.

00:09:18.384 --> 00:09:21.531
You want to influence
people by your mindset,

00:09:21.531 --> 00:09:22.364
by your heartset,

00:09:22.364 --> 00:09:24.401
you want to influence by
your thinking patterns.

00:09:24.401 --> 00:09:27.188
You want to influence
people by your productivity.

00:09:27.188 --> 00:09:29.669
So you want to influence people

00:09:29.669 --> 00:09:31.124
and that's the job of a leader.

00:09:31.124 --> 00:09:34.479
And then the third I of the
third I practice, impact.

00:09:34.479 --> 00:09:36.062
Less talk, more do.

00:09:37.865 --> 00:09:39.587
No matter what your title is,

00:09:39.587 --> 00:09:42.583
you know you're leading,
you know you're winning

00:09:42.583 --> 00:09:45.601
when you are creating impact.

00:09:45.601 --> 00:09:46.913
A lot of people in the world,

00:09:46.913 --> 00:09:48.292
and victims love to do this,

00:09:48.292 --> 00:09:50.338
they talk about what they're going to do.

00:09:50.338 --> 00:09:52.370
"Here's the project I'm going to launch.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.483
"Here's the marathon I'm going to run.

00:09:54.483 --> 00:09:57.093
"Here's the life I'm going to craft."

00:09:57.093 --> 00:09:58.565
But they don't execute.

00:09:58.565 --> 00:10:03.351
Impact is about translating
your ideas to execution.

00:10:03.351 --> 00:10:05.153
And so the fourth leadership lesson

00:10:05.153 --> 00:10:07.403
is be deep versus be light.

00:10:08.411 --> 00:10:09.244
Be deep.

00:10:10.427 --> 00:10:13.195
I mean, this is a gorgeous opportunity

00:10:13.195 --> 00:10:18.081
to build a monopoly of
mastery within your industry.

00:10:18.081 --> 00:10:20.005
I mean, you want to be
so good at what you do

00:10:20.005 --> 00:10:21.876
that when we watch you in action,

00:10:21.876 --> 00:10:24.326
tears come to our eyes.

00:10:24.326 --> 00:10:26.499
People rise to their feet and applaud you.

00:10:26.499 --> 00:10:27.846
And how do you do that?

00:10:27.846 --> 00:10:30.017
Well, you separate yourself

00:10:30.017 --> 00:10:32.420
from the way most people
operate in business,

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:33.424
and in life.

00:10:33.424 --> 00:10:34.452
You go deep.

00:10:34.452 --> 00:10:36.263
We live in a world that
is really suffering

00:10:36.263 --> 00:10:38.025
from the cult of superficiality.

00:10:38.025 --> 00:10:40.707
Everything is fast, everything is quick,

00:10:40.707 --> 00:10:42.846
everything is light.

00:10:42.846 --> 00:10:44.986
Imagine you resolve today,

00:10:44.986 --> 00:10:48.181
"I will be deep when I do work,

00:10:48.181 --> 00:10:49.385
"when I work on a project,

00:10:49.385 --> 00:10:51.533
"when I build a client relationship,

00:10:51.533 --> 00:10:52.918
"when I work with a personal relationship,

00:10:52.918 --> 00:10:55.051
"when I install a new habit."

00:10:55.051 --> 00:10:57.002
It's not going to be light.

00:10:57.002 --> 00:10:58.858
I'm not going to be superficial.

00:10:58.858 --> 00:11:00.662
I'm going to go deep.

00:11:00.662 --> 00:11:03.623
I'm going to bring rigor to my game.

00:11:03.623 --> 00:11:05.782
Let's say you're installing a new habit,

00:11:05.782 --> 00:11:07.466
let's say it's my famous 5:00 a.m. Club.

00:11:07.466 --> 00:11:09.860
You start reading all the literature

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:13.056
on the neurobiology of early rising.

00:11:13.056 --> 00:11:15.273
You actually read all of the books

00:11:15.273 --> 00:11:17.525
on habit installation.

00:11:17.525 --> 00:11:20.370
You actually find a coach, let's say,

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:23.720
to help you install the 5:00 a.m. routine.

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:27.461
You actually build out a
protocol to mark your progress.

00:11:27.461 --> 00:11:29.524
I'm just saying, could you imagine?

00:11:29.524 --> 00:11:31.732
Rather than going very wide in your work,

00:11:31.732 --> 00:11:33.988
or very wide in your personal life,

00:11:33.988 --> 00:11:37.297
you dial it in with a monomaniacal focus

00:11:37.297 --> 00:11:41.802
to be genius level at just
a few things, that's rigor.

00:11:41.802 --> 00:11:43.400
And rigor's really an approach.

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:47.444
I was in Luzern, Switzerland,
a few months ago.

00:11:47.444 --> 00:11:50.486
And I was working on my new book.

00:11:50.486 --> 00:11:53.412
And someone delivered some tea.

00:11:53.412 --> 00:11:55.047
And I had asked for some fresh lemon.

00:11:55.047 --> 00:11:59.115
And what I noticed is
whoever had sliced the lemons

00:11:59.115 --> 00:12:02.615
took the time to de-seed the lemon wedges.

00:12:03.561 --> 00:12:05.988
And that's really your
metaphor for this podcast.

00:12:05.988 --> 00:12:09.285
That's what this lesson is all about,

00:12:09.285 --> 00:12:10.921
the fourth leadership lesson.

00:12:10.921 --> 00:12:12.568
Be deep versus be light.

00:12:12.568 --> 00:12:15.020
It's about de-seeding the lemon wedges

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:17.992
on the areas of focus that
are most important to you.

00:12:17.992 --> 00:12:21.280
Anyone can just cut the lemon
wedges and hand them to you,

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:24.396
but this producer had the discipline,

00:12:24.396 --> 00:12:26.993
the bravery, the acumen,
the commitment, the devotion

00:12:26.993 --> 00:12:31.160
to actually take the time
to de-seed the lemon wedges.

00:12:33.101 --> 00:12:36.749
There is incredible
and indelible influence

00:12:36.749 --> 00:12:39.149
and imprinting going on on your philosophy

00:12:39.149 --> 00:12:41.532
and on your mind when you read a bood.

00:12:41.532 --> 00:12:43.663
So you have a conversation with someone,

00:12:43.663 --> 00:12:45.091
and I'm sure you've had a conversation,

00:12:45.091 --> 00:12:47.970
maybe it's with a chef
or a great entrepreneur

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:50.048
or a wise human being.

00:12:50.048 --> 00:12:53.458
And that maybe it was
just one conversation.

00:12:53.458 --> 00:12:57.263
But that person in that
10-minute conversation,

00:12:57.263 --> 00:13:00.097
or hour-long conversation
said something to you

00:13:00.097 --> 00:13:01.919
that formed your philosophy

00:13:01.919 --> 00:13:04.509
that changed the rest of your life.

00:13:04.509 --> 00:13:06.493
Well, that's what you can get in a book.

00:13:06.493 --> 00:13:09.150
You can have a conversation
tonight with Kanye,

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:14.016
you can have a conversation
the next night with Edison.

00:13:14.016 --> 00:13:17.067
You can have a conversation
two nights from now

00:13:17.067 --> 00:13:18.181
with Nelson Mandela.

00:13:18.181 --> 00:13:21.121
You can read the biography
that I recently read

00:13:21.121 --> 00:13:23.025
of Elon Musk.

00:13:23.025 --> 00:13:25.714
You can read about Steve Jobs.

00:13:25.714 --> 00:13:29.906
You can read about
Vincent van Gogh's life,

00:13:29.906 --> 00:13:34.073
you can read about Martin
Luther King, Jr.'s life.

00:13:35.557 --> 00:13:37.987
You can read about the Titans of Industry

00:13:37.987 --> 00:13:42.062
and what they went through
and how they innovated

00:13:42.062 --> 00:13:44.370
and how they dealt with struggles,

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:47.263
and how they dealt with wealth.

00:13:47.263 --> 00:13:49.106
You can read books on productivity

00:13:49.106 --> 00:13:51.138
from the most productive
people on the planet.

00:13:51.138 --> 00:13:54.227
You can learn how people
who build companies

00:13:54.227 --> 00:13:57.027
like Ikea and Red Bull, how they did it.

00:13:57.027 --> 00:14:00.417
They took a small idea and
built it into an empire.

00:14:00.417 --> 00:14:03.941
So what I'm suggesting to you,
with great love and respect,

00:14:03.941 --> 00:14:06.002
as I always do in these mastery sessions.

00:14:06.002 --> 00:14:10.146
Is reading a book is nothing
more than having a conversation

00:14:10.146 --> 00:14:11.492
with the author.

00:14:11.492 --> 00:14:15.483
And you can spend your evenings
or mornings or afternoons

00:14:15.483 --> 00:14:19.800
or lunch times connecting
with the greatest minds

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:21.488
who have ever graced the planet.

00:14:21.488 --> 00:14:23.206
You cannot put a price tag on that.

00:14:23.206 --> 00:14:26.477
If you hire C-level people,
please don't be surprised

00:14:26.477 --> 00:14:29.208
if you have a D-level company.

00:14:29.208 --> 00:14:31.254
If you truly want an iconic business,

00:14:31.254 --> 00:14:33.808
one that stands the
test of the generations,

00:14:33.808 --> 00:14:37.602
one where you build what I call
a globally cherished brand.

00:14:37.602 --> 00:14:41.769
Then you absolutely must make
it your dominant obsession

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:46.754
to hire and manage and retain and engage

00:14:46.754 --> 00:14:48.754
only A-level performers.

00:14:49.714 --> 00:14:53.905
The best leaders on the
planet grow other leaders.

00:14:53.905 --> 00:14:57.667
So if you're not growing
leaders faster than your peers,

00:14:57.667 --> 00:14:59.603
you're not leading, you're following.

00:14:59.603 --> 00:15:00.929
I'm going to repat that again.

00:15:00.929 --> 00:15:04.178
The job of the leader
is to grow more leaders.

00:15:04.178 --> 00:15:05.578
So, yes, do your work,

00:15:05.578 --> 00:15:06.959
yes, become more creative,

00:15:06.959 --> 00:15:08.366
yes, innovate,

00:15:08.366 --> 00:15:10.541
yes, optimize your personal life.

00:15:10.541 --> 00:15:12.463
And, yet, at the same time, make the time

00:15:12.463 --> 00:15:15.054
to develop leaders around you.

00:15:15.054 --> 00:15:16.910
What that might look like at the office

00:15:16.910 --> 00:15:18.478
celebrating a teammate.

00:15:18.478 --> 00:15:22.645
At the office, that might
look like modeling mastery.

00:15:23.522 --> 00:15:26.335
At the office it might
look like collaborating

00:15:26.335 --> 00:15:28.811
with someone who needs some collaboration.

00:15:28.811 --> 00:15:31.934
At home, what does
growing leaders look like?

00:15:31.934 --> 00:15:33.535
Well, it might be mentoring your children,

00:15:33.535 --> 00:15:36.473
exposing them to art,
giving them great books.

00:15:36.473 --> 00:15:40.241
It might mean being a positive
force in your home life.

00:15:40.241 --> 00:15:43.358
So the job of the leader
is to grow more leaders.

00:15:43.358 --> 00:15:45.725
One of the things I
observe is simply this.

00:15:45.725 --> 00:15:50.078
A lot of people are very
careless with their language.

00:15:50.078 --> 00:15:52.372
And so if there's a huge shift.

00:15:52.372 --> 00:15:53.371
You can either be a victim,

00:15:53.371 --> 00:15:55.310
or youc an be a leader,
but you can't be both.

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:57.934
And if you look at
someone who is a victim,

00:15:57.934 --> 00:16:01.804
a lot of their victim-hood comes down

00:16:01.804 --> 00:16:03.210
to the words they use.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:06.399
I mean, the words used
either lift up your energy.

00:16:06.399 --> 00:16:08.620
The words you use either
make you more creative.

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:11.929
The words you use either allow
you to do world class things.

00:16:11.929 --> 00:16:14.830
Or the words you use deny your talents.

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:17.164
The words you use could
reduce your energy.

00:16:17.164 --> 00:16:20.829
The words you use could
hurt your leadership.

00:16:20.829 --> 00:16:24.372
So you have a choice every
day, at work, and at home,

00:16:24.372 --> 00:16:25.763
and out on the streets with strangers.

00:16:25.763 --> 00:16:28.401
You can develop a victim vocabulary

00:16:28.401 --> 00:16:30.371
or the language of leadership.

00:16:30.371 --> 00:16:31.812
And in this mastery session,

00:16:31.812 --> 00:16:34.206
I want to walk you through
some of my insights on those.

00:16:34.206 --> 00:16:36.165
Well, words are powerful.

00:16:36.165 --> 00:16:39.461
I mean, words have destroyed nations.

00:16:39.461 --> 00:16:41.251
I mean, think about the great dictators,

00:16:41.251 --> 00:16:45.119
their words of hatred,
their words of toxicity,

00:16:45.119 --> 00:16:49.286
their words of breakdown
cause the people around them

00:16:50.367 --> 00:16:53.792
to do sometimes incredibly
terrifying acts.

00:16:53.792 --> 00:16:57.234
And then you look at people
like a Nelson Mandela

00:16:57.234 --> 00:17:00.282
or a Martin Luther King,
Jr., or a Mother Theresa.

00:17:00.282 --> 00:17:01.945
You look at the great business builders,

00:17:01.945 --> 00:17:03.370
you look at humanitarians,

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:05.323
you look at a lot of the great artists.

00:17:05.323 --> 00:17:07.038
And they were so careful with their words.

00:17:07.038 --> 00:17:10.163
And their words lifted people up.

00:17:10.163 --> 00:17:11.804
And that's really what great leaders do,

00:17:11.804 --> 00:17:14.253
they use the language of leadership.

00:17:14.253 --> 00:17:17.643
You look at a victim,
and they say things like,

00:17:17.643 --> 00:17:19.008
"This is a problem.

00:17:19.008 --> 00:17:20.430
"I've got a problem right here."

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:22.446
Or, "I'm really scared about this."

00:17:22.446 --> 00:17:25.085
Or, "I hate that client."

00:17:25.085 --> 00:17:26.430
Or, "I hate this work."

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:28.574
Or, "I'm sick of this day."

00:17:28.574 --> 00:17:30.780
Or, "I'm exhausted.

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:33.107
Or "I don't like that."

00:17:33.107 --> 00:17:34.533
Or "She never likes me."

00:17:34.533 --> 00:17:37.475
It's literally the language of toxicity.

00:17:37.475 --> 00:17:41.392
And the words you use
really are like a context

00:17:42.711 --> 00:17:45.843
or a framework, or a stained glass window

00:17:45.843 --> 00:17:47.135
on the way you see the world.

00:17:47.135 --> 00:17:49.376
I eman, here's a game-changing insight.

00:17:49.376 --> 00:17:51.233
You see the world not as it is.

00:17:51.233 --> 00:17:53.561
You see the world as you are.

00:17:53.561 --> 00:17:55.114
You see the world through a lens.

00:17:55.114 --> 00:17:59.248
And your language forms
that lens of belief.

00:17:59.248 --> 00:18:02.071
I mean, your lens of belief
and your personal filter

00:18:02.071 --> 00:18:06.030
on reality is not really
a true filter on reality,

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:10.257
it's just a filter on
reality that has formed

00:18:10.257 --> 00:18:12.571
as a result of all the
words you've picked up

00:18:12.571 --> 00:18:14.893
from the moment you were born.

00:18:14.893 --> 00:18:16.239
So when you were a little baby,

00:18:16.239 --> 00:18:17.551
your mom and your dad

00:18:17.551 --> 00:18:19.905
used the language that they were taught

00:18:19.905 --> 00:18:22.155
when they were kids to use.

00:18:23.373 --> 00:18:24.952
And if they used the
language of victimitis

00:18:24.952 --> 00:18:27.389
and if they use the
language of victimhood.

00:18:27.389 --> 00:18:31.281
Well, you know, you can't
do this, I hate this,

00:18:31.281 --> 00:18:34.220
I'm tired of this, money
doesn't grow on trees.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:35.566
Be ordinary.

00:18:35.566 --> 00:18:37.785
If you're successful, you'll be hated.

00:18:37.785 --> 00:18:40.560
All that messaging that
we pick from our parents

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:44.147
and the media and the world around us,

00:18:44.147 --> 00:18:44.980
and our peers, the little kids

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:46.198
that we used to play
with in the playground,

00:18:46.198 --> 00:18:48.851
or the friends that we had as teenagers.

00:18:48.851 --> 00:18:52.518
That will either lift
us up or tear us down.

00:18:53.924 --> 00:18:54.825
So I guess what I'm trying to say

00:18:54.825 --> 00:18:57.533
and hopefully I'm making
the point clearly for you,

00:18:57.533 --> 00:18:59.959
but we see the world
through this personal filter

00:18:59.959 --> 00:19:01.432
and a lot of this personal filter

00:19:01.432 --> 00:19:03.883
that drives our performance
and our behavior

00:19:03.883 --> 00:19:07.064
is really constructed from
the words that we've picked up

00:19:07.064 --> 00:19:08.725
and then the words we are taught to use.

00:19:08.725 --> 00:19:12.022
And so the right-angle turn for your

00:19:12.022 --> 00:19:16.189
to create exponential energy,
mindset, productivity,

00:19:17.110 --> 00:19:20.475
creativity, results in the
world, results in your life,

00:19:20.475 --> 00:19:24.459
comes down to, in part,
a really intelligent use

00:19:24.459 --> 00:19:25.629
of your words.

00:19:25.629 --> 00:19:28.906
And like I said at the beginning
of this mastery session,

00:19:28.906 --> 00:19:32.877
most people are really
unconscious about their words.

00:19:32.877 --> 00:19:36.360
And that's why most people are not getting

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:37.943
rare error results.

00:19:39.262 --> 00:19:41.293
So what are some of the
words I encourage you

00:19:41.293 --> 00:19:42.313
to shift from?

00:19:42.313 --> 00:19:44.150
Well, don't talk about
what you hate to do,

00:19:44.150 --> 00:19:45.771
talk about what you love to do.

00:19:45.771 --> 00:19:48.840
Don't talk about problems,
talk about opportunities.

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:50.872
Don't talk about what makes you tired,

00:19:50.872 --> 00:19:52.648
talk about what makes you inspired.

00:19:52.648 --> 00:19:56.698
Don't talk about your past,
talk about your future.

00:19:56.698 --> 00:19:59.417
Don't talk about what broke your heart,

00:19:59.417 --> 00:20:02.203
talk about what opened your heart.

00:20:02.203 --> 00:20:05.068
Don't talk about what dispirits you,

00:20:05.068 --> 00:20:07.901
talk about what you are fueled by.

00:20:09.097 --> 00:20:12.346
Don't talk about the
things within your life

00:20:12.346 --> 00:20:13.345
that are not working.

00:20:13.345 --> 00:20:16.503
Talk about the things in
your life that are working.

00:20:16.503 --> 00:20:20.891
Don't talk about your pain,
talk about your pleasure.

00:20:20.891 --> 00:20:24.027
And if I were sitting with you right now,

00:20:24.027 --> 00:20:25.881
coaching you, I mean, I am coaching you,

00:20:25.881 --> 00:20:28.137
and what I would encourage you to do

00:20:28.137 --> 00:20:29.578
as soon as a finish this mastery session

00:20:29.578 --> 00:20:31.181
is pull out your journal.

00:20:31.181 --> 00:20:33.852
And if you're not journaling,
please start journaling,

00:20:33.852 --> 00:20:36.640
because journaling is a
ritual you want to install

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:37.970
into your days.

00:20:37.970 --> 00:20:39.728
Joan Didion said this:

00:20:39.728 --> 00:20:43.559
"I don't know what I think
until I write it down."

00:20:43.559 --> 00:20:44.976
Think about that.

00:20:46.009 --> 00:20:49.507
"I don't know what I think
until I write it down."

00:20:49.507 --> 00:20:51.933
And so in your journal
after this mastery session,

00:20:51.933 --> 00:20:55.016
pull out a fresh page and draw a line

00:20:56.605 --> 00:20:58.076
down the center of the page.

00:20:58.076 --> 00:21:01.666
And write about the language of victimhood

00:21:01.666 --> 00:21:04.617
and the language of leadership.

00:21:04.617 --> 00:21:08.134
And start to write down
all the words yo use

00:21:08.134 --> 00:21:11.506
and the sentences you employ that really

00:21:11.506 --> 00:21:14.017
are, they're really victim-speak.

00:21:14.017 --> 00:21:14.961
And then in the other column,

00:21:14.961 --> 00:21:17.551
I want you to start writing
down the language of leadership,

00:21:17.551 --> 00:21:21.169
the language that as of
today you will be committed

00:21:21.169 --> 00:21:24.513
to using so that you
start to use the language

00:21:24.513 --> 00:21:26.199
of someone who's world class.

00:21:26.199 --> 00:21:27.860
And I've given you some examples,

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:30.914
but doing that exercise
will make it really clear.

00:21:30.914 --> 00:21:33.391
And as you know clarity breeds mastery.

00:21:33.391 --> 00:21:35.214
Bottom line is this.

00:21:35.214 --> 00:21:37.536
You are built to be a leader.

00:21:37.536 --> 00:21:40.752
You are not constructed to be a victim.

00:21:40.752 --> 00:21:43.140
And once your get your words right,

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:46.400
you are going to start
noticing daily shifts

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:49.185
in your energy, your focus, your momentum,

00:21:49.185 --> 00:21:51.600
your confidence and your output.

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:54.081
And when you start
getting those words right,

00:21:54.081 --> 00:21:56.018
you start lifting your professional life,

00:21:56.018 --> 00:21:58.834
your personal life to a
whole new level of wow.

00:21:58.834 --> 00:22:00.150
And, guess what?

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.160
That is what you are meant to do.

